From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kaonic Build <build@beechat.network>
Date: Thu, 18 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] spi: stm32: warn once, not per transfer, about st,spi-midi-ns

stm32h7_spi_data_idleness() uses spi->cur_midi for two different things:
the value read from the deprecated DT property, and the value currently in
force. Once a transfer's word_delay sets it, every later transfer sees a
non-zero cur_midi, concludes the value came from DT, and logs two warnings.

That is per transfer, not per device. Driving an AT86RF215 radio it amounts
to roughly a thousand messages a second, and on a serial console each costs
milliseconds - enough to make a transfer that should take microseconds take
tens of milliseconds, which looks like the inter-byte delay itself being
expensive.

The delay is needed for real here: the AT86RF215 requires 125 ns between
bytes (tSPI_5), so any SPI clock above 8 MHz has to insert it.

Warn once per device instead. Upstream reworked this area in v6.17; this is
the minimal fix for the 6.6 tree.

Signed-off-by: Kaonic Build <build@beechat.network>
---
 drivers/spi/spi-stm32.c | 7 ++++---
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/spi/spi-stm32.c b/drivers/spi/spi-stm32.c
--- a/drivers/spi/spi-stm32.c
+++ b/drivers/spi/spi-stm32.c
@@ -1813,10 +1813,11 @@ static void stm32h7_spi_data_idleness(struct stm32_spi *spi, struct spi_transfer
 	spi_delay_ns = spi_delay_to_ns(&xfer->word_delay, xfer);
 
 	if (spi->cur_midi != 0) {
-		dev_warn(spi->dev, "st,spi-midi-ns DT property is deprecated\n");
+		dev_warn_once(spi->dev, "st,spi-midi-ns DT property is deprecated\n");
 		if (spi_delay_ns) {
-			dev_warn(spi->dev, "Overriding st,spi-midi-ns with word_delay_ns %d\n",
-				 spi_delay_ns);
+			dev_warn_once(spi->dev,
+				      "Overriding st,spi-midi-ns with word_delay_ns %d\n",
+				      spi_delay_ns);
 				spi->cur_midi = spi_delay_ns;
 			}
 	} else {
